target/arm: WFE waits for the location an exclusive load monitors.

XNU's ticket and spin locks wait with LDXR/LDAXR; compare; WFE; retry (the
hottest kernel block of the run-357 swipe profile: __TEXT_EXEC+0x70b9c,
45M WFE and 45M exclusive loads in 14 s). The architecture wakes such a
WFE when the global monitor leaves Exclusive, i.e. when another observer
writes the monitored granule; QEMU's helper_wfe returns at once while the
monitor is armed, so the waiter re-runs its loop and leaves to the main
loop on every WFE for as long as the lock is held.

With the CPU property tomacco-wfe-wait-us (default 50, 0 = upstream) the
helper now waits, without BQL, until the monitored location differs from
what the load-exclusive read (checked first, so a store between the LDXR
and the WFE is not lost), SEV sets the event register (consumed), an
interrupt or kick is pending (checked at least every ~1-2 us, so exclusive
sections, queued work and cpu_exit are not held up), the event stream's
next event, or the bound. WFE may return spuriously, so an ABA store, a
location outside RAM or an unaligned or AArch32 exclusive only shorten the
wait. On an x86 host with WAITPKG each slice is UMONITOR/UMWAIT (C0.1) on
the location's cache line: the core idles and wakes on the store itself;
otherwise a PAUSE loop. A64 load-exclusives record their size in
env->tomacco_exclusive_bytes for the comparison. Single-threaded TCG
(round robin, icount) yields to the next vCPU instead of spinning out its
time slice.

tests/engine/wfe-monitor.S (virt, 2 CPUs, CPU 1 waits 200 us per round
for CPU 0's STLR): 4600-5200 WFEs per round -> 4 (one per 50 us bound),
release-to-acquire 0.4-1.9 us -> 0.5-0.9 us with UMWAIT (1.4 us with
PAUSE); a WFE on a never-written word returns after the bound (60 us), a
SEV wakes it, a word written before the WFE ends it at once.

